From c84ae2ff261d08bb212f5d72867185876d9d71e7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Penny <Pennyroyal@agentmail.to>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 14:35:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Do not require the ASAN header from a non-ASAN SM120 build

nvcc's host pass fails `common_memoryUtils.cuda.o` on any toolchain whose
compiler package does not install the sanitizer headers, because the file asked
for <sanitizer/asan_interface.h> unconditionally while using its macros only
under its own TLLM_HAS_ASAN detection. Fedora 44's gcc-15.3.1 package is one such
toolchain, and the same package set backs the published image, so the carried
SM120 build could not compile this translation unit at all.

The header is now asked for under the same condition the macros are used under.
The condition is restated rather than reusing TLLM_HAS_ASAN, because the file
defines that macro inside the namespace it opens, and a system header does not
belong inside a namespace; the two branches are copied verbatim from the
detection directly below, so they cannot drift without the macros disappearing.

An ASAN-enabled build is unchanged: it still requires, and still receives, the
real header. Nothing else in the file moves -- no stub header, no widened include
path, no arithmetic, dispatch or toolchain change. Paths are relative to the
site-packages directory the step is installing into, rather than the absolute
site-packages paths the two accepted mailboxes name, so the step keeps working
wherever the selected interpreter puts its packages.
---
 .../data/csrc/nv_internal/cpp/common/memoryUtils.cu       | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/flashinfer/data/csrc/nv_internal/cpp/common/memoryUtils.cu b/flashinfer/data/csrc/nv_internal/cpp/common/memoryUtils.cu
index 48b7df9..4f71988 100644
--- a/flashinfer/data/csrc/nv_internal/cpp/common/memoryUtils.cu
+++ b/flashinfer/data/csrc/nv_internal/cpp/common/memoryUtils.cu
@@ -15,7 +15,15 @@
  */
 
 #include <curand_kernel.h>
+// ASAN_POISON_MEMORY_REGION and ASAN_UNPOISON_MEMORY_REGION are used below only
+// under this file's own TLLM_HAS_ASAN detection, and the ASAN interface header
+// provides nothing else this translation unit needs. Compiler packages do not
+// universally install that header, so ask for it under the same condition the
+// macros are used under. An ASAN-enabled build still requires the real header.
+#if defined(__SANITIZE_ADDRESS__) || \
+    (defined(__has_feature) && __has_feature(address_sanitizer))
 #include <sanitizer/asan_interface.h>
+#endif
 #include <sys/stat.h>
 
 #include <unordered_map>
